<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S2.T2.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.T2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:113%;">Benchmark Comparison.<span id="S2.T2.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Benchmarks are compared by image count, contextual incongruity, LLM-free evaluation, and whether both missed and hallucinated object recognitions are assessed.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The ORIC benchmark</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section introduces ORIC, a benchmark for evaluating LVLMs’ object recognition under contextual incongruity. Formulated as a binary classification task, it assigns each question a “yes” or “no” label based on the target object’s presence. As shown in Tab. <a href="#S2.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 2.2 Quantitative Analysis of Object-Background Associations via CLIPScore ‣ 2 Revisiting POPE: The Role of Contextual Incongruity in Object Recognition ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, ORIC uniquely combines contextual incongruity and LLM-free evaluation while assessing both missed and hallucinated recognitions, setting it apart from existing benchmarks. We outline our question construction, statistical analysis, and evaluation protocol.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>ORIC Construction Method</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Positive Question (Existing Objects):</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Contextual incongruity often arises when objects appear in unexpected settings, increasing misidentification risk. Therefore, our objective is to generate questions that deliberately minimize background-object associations, utilizing <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-guided sampling</span>. We define the objects targeted for recognition as ROI (regions of interest), while background contexts consist of non-ROI elements. Formally, as illustrated on the left side of Fig. <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.2 Quantitative Analysis of Object-Background Associations via CLIPScore ‣ 2 Revisiting POPE: The Role of Contextual Incongruity in Object Recognition ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, given an image <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> containing objects <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{O}=\{o_{i}=(n_{i},\{B_{ij}\}_{j=1}^{m_{i}})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}=\{o_{i}=(n_{i},\{B_{ij}\}_{j=1}^{m_{i}})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the object’s name and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th bounding box associated with object <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we categorize objects into ROI and non-ROI based on their bounding box coverage. We then select <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> ROI objects as positive question candidates, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired number of selected objects. The total area covered by each object’s bounding boxes is calculated as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=\mathrm{area}\Bigl(\bigcup_{j=1}^{m_{i}}B_{ij}\Bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>area</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munderover><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=\mathrm{area}\Bigl(\bigcup_{j=1}^{m_{i}}B_{ij}\Bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where the function <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{area}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>area</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{area}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> computes pixel area, and then we split <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> into two disjoint sets based on the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50th" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50th</annotation></semantics></math> percentile: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{ROI}}=\{o_{(i)}\mid A_{(i)}&lt;P_{50}(A)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext>ROI</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>50</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{ROI}}=\{o_{(i)}\mid A_{(i)}&lt;P_{50}(A)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{nonROI}}=\{o_{(i)}\mid A_{(i)}\geq P_{50}(A)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext>nonROI</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>50</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{nonROI}}=\{o_{(i)}\mid A_{(i)}\geq P_{50}(A)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{50}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>50</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{50}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the median (50th percentile) of the bounding box coverage. We then use GPT-4o as LLM to filter ROI candidates. Specifically, the LLM is queried to determine whether each ROI object is logically consistent with the provided non-ROI object categories. The verification function is defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(o)=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }\text{LLM}(o,\mathcal{O}_{\text{nonROI}})=\text{``no''},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mtext>LLM</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext>nonROI</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>“no”</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(o)=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if }\text{LLM}(o,\mathcal{O}_{\text{nonROI}})=\text{``no''},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The function <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{LLM}(o,\mathcal{O}_{\text{nonROI}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>LLM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext>nonROI</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{LLM}(o,\mathcal{O}_{\text{nonROI}})</annotation></semantics></math> returns “no” if the ROI object is unexpected based on common sense and typical co-occurrence. Objects receiving a ”no” from GPT-4o form the positive candidate set <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Positive questions are generated by randomly selecting <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> objects from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. For detailed pseudocode and prompts, refer to Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 LLM-Guided Sampling Method (Positive Question Construction) ‣ Appendix A ORIC Construction, Analysis, and Evaluation Metrics ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Negative Question (Nonexistent Objects):</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LVLMs are prone to object hallucinations when strong contextual cues make nonexistent objects appear plausible, resulting in incongruous contexts. Therefore, our goal is to generate questions that enhance the correlation between nonexistent ROI objects and non-ROI elements by leveraging <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CLIP-guided sampling</span>. As depicted on the right side of Fig. <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.2 Quantitative Analysis of Object-Background Associations via CLIPScore ‣ 2 Revisiting POPE: The Role of Contextual Incongruity in Object Recognition ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we first identify the most visually similar image <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to a query image <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> using the CLIP model’s image encoder. Formally, Given images <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I_{1},\dots,I_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I_{1},\dots,I_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> a query image <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, visual embeddings are extracted via ViT: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=ViT(I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=ViT(I)</annotation></semantics></math>. The image similarity is measured using cosine distance:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(I_{q},I_{i})=1-\frac{\mathbf{e}_{q}\cdot\mathbf{e}_{i}}{\|\mathbf{e}_{q}\|\|\mathbf{e}_{i}\|}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(I_{q},I_{i})=1-\frac{\mathbf{e}_{q}\cdot\mathbf{e}_{i}}{\|\mathbf{e}_{q}\|\|\mathbf{e}_{i}\|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represent embeddings of image <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The most similar image <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes this distance. Next, given the most similar image <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and a set of nonexistent ROI objects <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{non}}=\{n_{i}\}^{M}_{i=1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext>non</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{non}}=\{n_{i}\}^{M}_{i=1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents an individual nonexistent ROI object and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of nonexistent ROI objects considered in the set <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{non}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext>non</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{non}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, a text description <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated in the form of ”<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">an image contains <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></span>”. We compute the similarity score for each object as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=\text{CLIPScore}(I^{\prime},T_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>CLIPScore</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=\text{CLIPScore}(I^{\prime},T_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. The objects are then sorted by <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the top <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> nonexistent ROI objects are selected to form <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{non}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext>non</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{non}}</annotation></semantics></math> for negative question generation. See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 CLIP-Guided Sampling Method (Negative Question Construction) ‣ Appendix A ORIC Construction, Analysis, and Evaluation Metrics ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> for the detailed algorithm.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>ORIC Statistics</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Human Evaluation:</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We sampled 150 “yes” and 150 “no” questions from ORIC and manually checked <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> object labeling and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> visual context incongruity. The low <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\%</annotation></semantics></math> error rate confirms the robustness of our pipeline. Appendix <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Error Questions from Human Evaluation ‣ Appendix D Visualization of ORIC Examples ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> lists six errors; more examples are in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 ORIC Question Examples ‣ Appendix D Visualization of ORIC Examples ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">CLIPScore for ROI–Background Analysis:</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compared ORIC-generated questions with incongruous context questions in Sec.<a href="#S2" title="2 Revisiting POPE: The Role of Contextual Incongruity in Object Recognition ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> using a CLIPScore-based method. Specifically, we generated 50 ORIC questions (25 for each label, “yes” and “no”) corresponding to the same images used in the previous incongruous context questions. As illustrated in Fig.<a href="#S1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(c), CLIP scores for “yes” questions were nearly identical between ORIC (20.77) and incongruous context questions (20.63), suggesting similar contextual alignment. However, for “no” questions, ORIC achieved higher CLIP scores (24.25 vs. 22.87), indicating a stronger correlation between the nonexistent object and the visual context, thereby creating a more incongruous context.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Image Similarity Analysis via Minimum Distance:</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further characterize the ORIC, we analyzed the visual relationships between positive and negative questions through image similarity measurements. Specifically, for each object class appearing in positive (“yes”) questions, we computed its minimum visual distance to negative (“no”) questions containing the same object class. Given an object <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, let the set of positive images be <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{i}^{+}=\{I_{i,1}^{+},\dots,I_{i,m}^{+}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{i}^{+}=\{I_{i,1}^{+},\dots,I_{i,m}^{+}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the set of negative images be <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{i}^{-}=\{I_{i,1}^{-},\dots,I_{i,n}^{-}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{i}^{-}=\{I_{i,1}^{-},\dots,I_{i,n}^{-}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We extracted visual feature vectors using a ViT encoder and computed pairwise cosine distances as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(I^{+}_{i,k},I^{-}_{i,l})=1-\frac{e(I^{+}_{i,k})\cdot e(I^{-}_{i,l})}{\|e(I^{+}_{i,k})\|\,\|e(I^{-}_{i,l})\|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(I^{+}_{i,k},I^{-}_{i,l})=1-\frac{e(I^{+}_{i,k})\cdot e(I^{-}_{i,l})}{\|e(I^{+}_{i,k})\|\,\|e(I^{-}_{i,l})\|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(\cdot)=\text{ViT}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>ViT</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(\cdot)=\text{ViT}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the ViT feature extractor. The minimum distance between positive and negative sets is defined as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\min}=\min_{k,l}D(I^{+}_{i,k},I^{-}_{i,l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\min}=\min_{k,l}D(I^{+}_{i,k},I^{-}_{i,l})</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure thorough evaluation, we calculated these minimum distances using three widely used vision encoders commonly employed in encoder-based LVLMs: CLIP-ViT-BigG-P14, SigLIP-SO400M-P14-384 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>, and EVA02-CLIP-BigE-P14 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>. These analyses highlight the distinctiveness of ORIC in capturing contextually challenging object recognition scenarios compared to existing benchmarks. In Tab. <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Image Similarity Analysis via Minimum Distance: ‣ 3.2 ORIC Statistics ‣ 3 The ORIC benchmark ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, ORIC shows consistently smaller minimum cosine distances between ”yes” and ”no” samples than POPE across all three vision encoders. This suggests greater visual similarity between positive and negative examples, making object recognition more challenging and realistic.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T3.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T3.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
