<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T6.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.T6.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:113%;">Model Performance on POPE vs. ORIC.<span id="S4.T6.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Comparison of POPE and ORIC, both with 2000 balanced questions across five models. We report macro precision (Prec.), recall (Rec.), and F1 score (F1) for four LVLMs and one detection model, showing that ORIC poses a greater challenge.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Beyond POPE, Increased Challenge:</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the impact of contextual incongruity, we compare ORIC with the prior POPE benchmark, both of which contain a balanced set of 2,000 questions. As shown in Tab. <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ ORIC Ablation Study: ‣ 4.2 Supplementary Experiments and Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments and Analysis ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, five models that performed well on POPE show significantly lower macro F1 scores on our benchmark, with performance gaps across models becoming more pronounced. Notably, GPT-4o-0806 exhibits the smallest macro F1 drop (11.3), highlighting its robustness. A full comparison across 18 LVLMs and two open-vocabulary detectors is provided in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Full Results of Comparison between POPE and ORIC ‣ Appendix B Experiment and Analysis ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Performance Comparison Across Object Sizes:</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt COCO’s size tiers—small (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;24^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>24</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;24^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> pt<sup id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>), medium (24–96<sup id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> pt<sup id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>), and large (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 96^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>96</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 96^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> pt<sup id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>)—to compare POPE and ORIC on 1,000 “yes”-labeled questions from each. All four models exhibit lower recall on ORIC, with an average drop of 13.51 points across sizes. Emu3‑Chat performs worst, particularly on small objects (<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="68.22\rightarrow 38.73" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>68.22</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>38.73</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">68.22\rightarrow 38.73</annotation></semantics></math>, –29.49), while GPT‑4o‑0806 is most robust on large ones (–7.59). Notably, Emu3‑Chat’s large–small gap widens from 25.97 in POPE to 33.26 in ORIC, and InternVL3‑9B’s from 14.05 to 22.82. Although large objects remain easier to recognize, the consistent decline across all sizes suggests that incongruous context—rather than object scale—is the primary cause of performance degradation. Object size distributions for POPE, ORIC, and COCO are shown in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Comparison of Object Size Distribution between POPE, ORIC, and COCO ‣ Appendix B Experiment and Analysis ‣ ORIC: Benchmarking Object Recognition in Incongruous Context for Large Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
